[00:38.21]Conversation One
[00:40.12]W: So, Mike, you manage the innovation project at CucinTech.
[00:45.20]M: I did indeed.
[00:47.24]W: Well then, first, congratulations. 
[00:49.98]It seems to have been very successful.
[00:53.10]M: Thanks. Yes, I really help things turn around at CucinTech.
[00:58.73]W: Was the revival in their fortunes entirely due to strategic innovation?
[01:04.00]M: Yes, yes, I think it was. 
[01:07.50]CucinTech was a company who was very much following the pack, doing what everyone else was doing and getting rapidly left behind. 
[01:16.94]I could see there was a lot of talent there, and some great potential, particularly in their product development. 
[01:24.68]I just had to harness that somehow.
[01:27.70]W: Was innovation at the core of the project?
[01:30.86]M: Absolutely. If it doesn't sound like too much of a clich¨¦, our world is constantly changing and it's changing quickly. 
[01:40.45]We need to be innovating constantly to keep up with this. 
[01:45.14]Stand still and you're lost.
[01:47.91]W: No stopping to sniff the roses?
[01:50.53]M: Well, I'll do that in my personal life, sure. 
[01:54.58]But as a business strategy, I'm afraid there is no stopping.
[01:59.57]W: What exactly is strategic innovation then?
[02:03.22]M: Strategic innovation is the process of managing innovation, of making sure it takes place at all levels of the company, and that is related to the company's overall strategy.
[02:16.05]W: I see.
[02:17.30]M: So, instead of innovation for innovation's sake and new products being created simply because the technology is there, the company culture must switch from these point-in-time innovations to continuous pipeline of innovations from everywhere and everyone.
[02:36.49]W: How did you align strategies throughout the company?
[02:39.98]M: I soon became aware that campaigning is useless. 
[02:44.37]People take no notice. 
[02:46.80]Simply, it came about through good practice trickling down. 
[02:51.40]This built consent. 
[02:53.47]People could see it was the best way to work.
[02:56.91]W: Does innovation on the skill really give a competitive advantage?
[03:01.56]M: I am certain of it, absolutely, especially if it's difficult for a competitor to copy. 
[03:09.17]The risk is of course that innovation may frequently lead to imitation.
[03:14.84]W: But not if it's strategic?
[03:16.75]M: Precisely.
[03:18.42]W: Thanks for talking to us.
[03:20.25]M: Sure.
[03:22.17]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[03:28.43]1. What seems to have been very successful according to the woman speaker?
[03:47.55]2. What did the company lack before the man's scheme was implemented?
[04:07.91]3. What does the man say he should do in his business?
[04:26.55]4. What does the man say is the risk of innovation?
[04:44.66]Conversation Two
[04:48.06]M: Today my guest is Dayna Ivanovich who has worked for the last twenty years as an interpreter. 
[04:54.65]Dayna, welcome.
[04:56.38]W: Thank you.
[04:57.94]M: Now, I'd like to begin by saying that I have on occasions used an interpreter myself, as a foreign correspondent. 
[05:06.29]So I am full of admiration for what you do. 
[05:09.87]But I think your profession is sometimes underrated, and many people think anyone who speaks more than one language can do it.
[05:20.09]W: There aren't any interpreters I know who don't have professional qualifications and training.
[05:26.23]You only really get proficient after many years in the job.
[05:30.71]M: And am I right in saying you can divide what you do into two distinct methods¡ªsimultaneous and consecutive interpreting?
[05:40.63]W: That's right. 
[05:41.95]The techniques you use are different, and a lot of interpreters will say one is easier than the other, less stressful.
[05:50.27]M: Simultaneous interpreting, putting someone's words into another language more or less as they speak, sounds to me like the more difficult.
[05:59.81]W: Well, actually no. 
[06:01.91]Most people in the business would agree that consecutive interpreting is the more stressful. 
[06:08.76]You have to wait for the speaker to deliver quite a chunk of language before you then put it into the second language, which puts your short-term memory under intense stress.
[06:21.28]M: You make notes, I presume.
[06:23.82]W: Absolutely, anything like numbers, names, places have to be noted down. 
[06:30.67]But the rest is never translated word for word. 
[06:34.14]You have to find a way of summarizing it, so that the message is there. 
[06:39.32]Turning every single word into the target language would put too much strain on the interpreter and slow down the whole process too much.
[06:49.15]M: But, with simultaneous interpreting, you start translating almost as soon as the other person starts speaking. 
[06:57.18]You must have some preparation beforehand.
[07:00.25]W: Well, hopefully the speakers will let you have an outline of the topic a day or two in advance. 
[07:07.18]You have a little time to do research, prepare technical expressions and so on.
[07:13.39]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[07:20.11]5. What are the speakers mainly talking about?
[07:38.92]6. What does the man think of Dayna's profession?
[07:57.01]7. What does Dayna say about the interpreters she knows?
[08:15.92]8. What do most interpreters think of consecutive interpreting?
